## Observability Conventions

### Metric Naming

**Prometheus (snake_case):**
- Format: `{namespace}_{subsystem}_{name}_{unit}` — e.g., `http_server_requests_total`, `db_query_duration_seconds`
- Counters must end with `_total`
- Use base units only: `_seconds` (not `_ms`), `_bytes` (not `_kb`), `_ratio` (not `_percent`)
- No type abbreviations in name: not `http_requests_counter_total`

**Datadog (dot.notation):**
- Format: `{service}.{subsystem}.{name}` — e.g., `order.service.requests`, `order.db.query.duration`
- Tags replace label dimensions — keep tag keys lowercase with underscores

### Cardinality Limits

- [CRITICAL] Labels/tags with unbounded values: user IDs, request IDs, raw URLs, session tokens, IP addresses
- [CRITICAL] Datadog custom metric exceeding 350 unique tag combinations
- [HIGH] Any label with more than 50 distinct values without documented justification
- Safe label values: HTTP method, status code class (2xx/4xx/5xx), route template, environment, region

**Cardinality math:** `unique_series = product(distinct_values_per_label)`. Budget before adding labels.

### Structured Logging

Required JSON fields on every log line:
```
timestamp   — ISO 8601 UTC
level       — error | warn | info | debug
message     — human-readable string
service     — service name
```

Trace correlation fields:
- **OTel:** `trace_id` (32 hex chars), `span_id` (16 hex chars), `trace_flags`
- **Datadog:** `dd.trace_id` (decimal integer), `dd.span_id` (decimal integer), `dd.service`, `dd.env`, `dd.version`

Do not mix OTel hex format with Datadog decimal format in the same log pipeline.

### Span Naming

- Use route template, not the actual path: `GET /users/{id}` not `GET /users/12345`
- gRPC: `{ServiceName}/{MethodName}` — e.g., `OrderService/CreateOrder`
- Database: `{db.operation.name} {db.name}` — e.g., `SELECT orders`
- Background jobs: `{job.type} {resource}` — e.g., `process Invoice`
- No user-controlled data in span names (cardinality + security)

### Health Endpoints

- Every service must expose `/livez` (liveness) and `/readyz` (readiness)
- `/livez`: returns 200 if process is alive — no dependency checks
- `/readyz`: returns 200 if ready for traffic, 503 if any required dependency is unavailable
- Both endpoints must be excluded from distributed tracing and APM
- Response body must be JSON with `status` field: `{"status": "ok"}` or `{"status": "degraded", "checks": {}}`

### Alerting Standards

- Alert on symptoms (error rate, latency, availability) — never on causes (CPU, memory, disk unless > 95%)
- Every monitor/alert must include a `runbook` URL
- Use multi-window burn rate: fast burn (1h window, 14x rate → page), slow burn (6h window, 5x rate → ticket)
- `notify_no_data: true` only for metrics guaranteed to always emit during normal operation
